# ad10 — Remote Reach in Minutes (hero cut, ~97s)
# Master plan: marketing/assets/videos/remote-reach.md
#
# Total segments: 10

[00:00.0 --> 00:02.6]
Here's the whole setup -- on a real clock.

[00:02.6 --> 00:11.2]
One brew install. One self-contained binary -- the message broker is embedded. No runtime to stage, no gateway daemon, no pairing.

[00:11.2 --> 00:15.4]
Run it, and the terminal itself is the platform.

[00:15.4 --> 00:32.9]
Your agent is a markdown file -- persona in the body, channels in the frontmatter, secrets as env tokens that never touch the file. Spawn it, start its Slack channel... connected.

[00:32.9 --> 00:44.4]
A teammate asks on Slack. The agent answers from its skill file -- a real reply, on the channel your team already uses. Email and website chat ride the same engine.

[00:44.4 --> 00:52.8]
Under two minutes in: share the agent's terminal, live. One command -- and any session you authorize can watch this pane.

[00:52.8 --> 01:07.2]
A second rysh session subscribes and watches the same pane, live -- that's the owner's terminal, mirrored. View or control: you decide, per share.

[01:07.2 --> 01:16.1]
And managing it doesn't need a seat at the box. Any shell injects commands headlessly with rysh send...

[01:16.1 --> 01:31.5]
...or attaches to the running session: stop the Slack channel, unshare the pane, detach. The session keeps running. Start, stop, govern -- from wherever you are.

[01:31.5 --> 01:36.6]
One binary. One markdown file. Reachable from everywhere. rysh, at rysh dot A I.
